---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1><span class="tick">▲</span> keel</h1>
      <p class="lede"><strong>A keel is the beam a hull is built on</strong> — laid first,
      under everything, keeping the vessel steady without ever being the boat. That is
      exactly what this is to <a href="https://code.claude.com">Claude Code</a>: a thin,
      durable layer underneath that holds your sessions steady and your setup on course.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/install">install in two lines</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/JimmayVV/keel">source ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="waterline" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 960 150" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <line class="water" x1="0" y1="38" x2="960" y2="38" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 7" opacity="0.7"/>
          <path class="hull" d="M180 38 C 260 118, 700 118, 780 38" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect class="fin" x="474" y="100" width="12" height="42" rx="6"/>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">what's in the box</span>
      <h2>Guardrails, memory wiring, and a record of your work</h2>
      <p class="sub">Everything rides on surfaces Anthropic documents — so it survives the
      platform's weekly releases, and tearing any of it out is one command that leaves no
      residue.</p>
      <div class="grid cols3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>ingest boundary</h3>
          <p>Every web and MCP result wrapped as <strong>data, never instructions</strong> —
          closing the indirect prompt-injection path where a poisoned page tells your agent
          to exfiltrate, install, or approve something. Read-only: it never blocks a call,
          never mutates a result.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>security guard</h3>
          <p>Blocks irreversible catastrophe, asks before exfiltration-shaped commands —
          and knows a Dockerfile that mentions <code>rm -rf</code> is text, not code.
          Fails closed on a broken policy, and your own rules can exempt any shipped one.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>commit hygiene</h3>
          <p>Blocks attribution trailers you never want in history. A preference a model
          will forget, because it's one line competing with a hundred others — a hook
          doesn't forget.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>activity log</h3>
          <p>What you asked and what was concluded, tagged by repo and branch — so
          research, review, and dead ends that never produce a commit are still
          recoverable weeks later. No model calls, documented payloads only.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>memory wiring</h3>
          <p>Third-party memory backends bolted on over documented MCP surfaces. keel owns
          the interface; engines are swappable. It ships no vector store, no LLM call, no
          daemon of its own.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>five skills</h3>
          <p><code>week</code> · <code>deck</code> · <code>telos</code> ·
          <code>guide</code> · <code>setup</code> — what happened, what's promised, what
          it's all for, how it works, and how it's wired.
          <a href="/skills">The tour →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">install</span>
      <h2>Two lines. Vanilla or years-old setup, same two lines.</h2>
      <pre><code><span class="p">/plugin</span> marketplace add JimmayVV/keel
<span class="p">/plugin</span> install keel@keel</code></pre>
      <p class="sub">That is the whole install — a normal plugin, additive by construction.
      Then <code>/keel:setup</code> walks the configuration as a conversation, asking only
      about the gaps. <a href="/install">The full story, including the teardown →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">the rules</span>
      <h2>Small on purpose, with a written constitution</h2>
      <div style="margin-top:1.6rem">
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">01</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Own your config, buy your capabilities</h3>
            <p>Guardrails and setup are configuration — personal, a few hundred lines,
            correctly yours. Memory and recall are capabilities — already built better by
            people who do it full time. keel wires them in. It does not reimplement them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">02</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Documented surfaces only</h3>
            <p>If Anthropic hasn't documented it, keel doesn't read, write, or parse it.
            The undocumented surfaces it does touch are three repairs, listed in the open
            with their blast radius and exit conditions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">03</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Admission by felt need</h3>
            <p>Prose is free. A test must have caught a failure that already occurred.
            Runtime requires a need felt at least once, by a real user, on a real machine.
            This is the rule that keeps a harness from becoming a second product.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><a href="/philosophy">Why it's shaped this way →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">honestly</span>
      <h2>A personal tool, built in the open</h2>
      <p class="sub">v0.4. Issues and PRs welcome and may be politely declined; fork
      freely — that's what the licence is for. It is opinionated in the way only a tool
      with a single demanding user can be, and those opinions are written down where you
      can disagree with them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
